'''Liang Cavall-Shen''' (born 29 August 2005) is a Balboan professional footballer who plays as a [[Forward (association football)|striker]] for [[Club Esportiu Sant Bart]] in [[La Lliga de l'Istme|La Lliga]] and the [[Balboa national football team]]. The son of former Balboan women's football star [[Bintu Cavall]] and Zong-Balboan multimedia artist Shen Wei-Lin, and the nephew of [[Nèstir Cavall]] — the greatest Balboan male footballer of all time — Liang is the latest product of the [[Cavall family]] footballing dynasty and one of the most highly regarded young players in Balboan football.

== Early life ==

Liang was born in [[Sant Miquel (ciutat)|Sant Miquel]], [[Sant Cristòfor]], Balboa, to [[Bintu Cavall]], the former Balboan women's international and current head coach of the [[Balboa women's national football team]], and Shen Wei-Lin, a Zong-Balboan multimedia artist. His heritage spans the full breadth of Balboan society: Volisanian-Balboan and Black Balboan through his mother's side, and Zong-Balboan through his father's side. He is the grandson of Sergi Cavall, a set builder for the Sant Cristòfor film studios, and Adama Rovira, a chef in [[Original (ciutat)|Original]], Sant Cristòfor.

Liang grew up immersed in football. His mother was still playing professionally when he was a young child — she retired from [[Club Esportiu Sant Bart]] in 2012, when Liang was seven — and his uncle [[Nèstir Cavall]] was the most famous athlete in Balboan history. The Cavall household was a place where football was discussed, debated, and lived every day, and Liang's talent was recognised early by both his mother and his uncle.

Despite his Sant Cristòfor roots, Liang joined the [[Club Esportiu Sant Bart]] youth academy at the age of twelve — a decision that surprised some observers, given the family's association with the corridor clubs. The move was influenced by Bintu's own experience at Sant Bart, where she had spent the final years of her playing career and where the women's and youth development programmes were among the strongest in the country. Liang has said that his mother's connection to the club made the decision natural: "Mum always said Sant Bart gave her the chance to prove herself when the Mercat couldn't anymore. I wanted to follow that path."

== Club career ==

=== Club Esportiu Sant Bart (2023–present) ===

Liang progressed rapidly through the Sant Bart youth system, earning a reputation as a clinical finisher with a maturity beyond his years. He was promoted to the first-team squad for the 2023 Clausura at the age of seventeen and made his La Lliga debut on 12 August 2023, coming on as a substitute in a 2–1 victory over [[FC Migjorn Sud]].

His breakthrough came in the 2025 Apertura, when he established himself as a regular starter and scored six goals in fifteen appearances, including a memorable brace in the [[Clàssic de l'Istme]] against [[FC Sant Cristòfor]] — the club where his uncle Nèstir had begun his career. The goals, scored in front of the Sant Bart ultras at the [[Estadi Nacional]], announced Liang as a talent of genuine promise and drew inevitable comparisons to his uncle.

Liang's playing style — powerful, direct, and physically imposing — has drawn comparisons not to Nèstir's creative flair but to his mother Bintu's directness and finishing instinct. He is strongest in the penalty area, where his movement, aerial ability, and composure in front of goal recall the great Sant Bart strikers of the post-reunification era. At 1.82 m, he is significantly taller than either his uncle or his mother, and his physical presence has made him an effective target man in Sant Bart's attacking system.

== International career ==

Liang received his first call-up to the [[Balboa national football team|Balboa national team]] in September 2025, at the age of nineteen, for the [[2026 AFA World Cup]] qualifying campaign. He made his debut as a substitute in a 3–0 victory over [[Dagit]] and earned his second cap the following month against [[Sarta]]. He was named to Balboa's 26-player squad for the [[2026 AFA World Cup]].

His selection for the World Cup squad — at the expense of more experienced forwards — was a statement of intent by manager [[Miquel Xifra II]], who has publicly stated that Liang represents the future of Balboan attacking football. The decision was not without controversy: some commentators argued that Liang's inclusion was influenced by the Cavall name rather than merit, a charge that Liang and his mother have both dismissed.

== Personal life ==

Liang is the eldest son of Bintu Cavall and Shen Wei-Lin. His younger brother, Kèma Cavall-Shen (born 2010), is also a footballer and currently plays in the Sant Bart youth system. His uncle is [[Nèstir Cavall]], the former Balboan international and 2006 World Cup winner, and his cousin [[Nèstir Cavall Jr.]] plays as a midfielder for [[Castejón FC]] and the Balboa national team.

Liang has been the subject of intense media attention since his debut, owing to the Cavall family name and the weight of expectation that comes with it. He has handled the scrutiny with a composure that observers have attributed to his upbringing in a family of professional athletes. In a 2025 interview, he said: "The name opens doors, but it also puts a target on your back. I don't want to be Nèstir Cavall's nephew or Bintu Cavall's son on the pitch — I want to be Liang Cavall. Everything else is just noise."

